# Task: Refactor the Svelte frontend to React

## Context
This is an existing web-app prototype: a Go backend with a Svelte frontend.
The backend stays as-is. I'm replacing the Svelte frontend with a React frontend
so the UI interoperates cleanly with **Claude Design**, which I'll use to design
and generate components from here on. React is the target specifically because
Claude Design emits React, so I want the codebase shaped to receive that output
with minimal friction.

## Read these first — do NOT write code yet
- `spec.md`        — product spec / functional requirements
- `claude.md`      — project conventions and working notes
- `ui.md`          — UI spec and design intent
- `docs/REAC_MIGRATION.md` — the refactor plan I've already written; follow it
- `frontend/design-ref/` — UI references exported from Claude
  Design, copied into this feature branch

After reading, summarize back to me:
1. Your understanding of what the app does and its current Svelte structure.
2. How the refactor plan sequences the work.
3. Any contradictions or gaps you see between the docs.
Wait for my confirmation before starting.

## Goal & hard constraints
- Achieve **feature and behavior parity** with the current Svelte frontend.
- **Do not change the Go backend or its API contracts.** Match the existing
  request/response shapes, routes, auth, and error handling exactly. If you
  think the API needs to change, stop and ask.
- Follow `docs/REAC_MIGRATION.md`. Where you'd deviate, explain why and get my OK first.
- Work **incrementally** — one route/feature at a time, keeping the app runnable.
  Don't do a big-bang rewrite in a single pass.

## Stack (confirm before scaffolding)
- React + [Vite] for the build, [TypeScript], [Tailwind] for styling.
  I'm choosing these to match what Claude Design produces so its components drop
  in with little rework. If you'd recommend otherwise given the references, say so.

## Claude Design handoff — this needs to be durable
I'll be bouncing between Claude Design (to design/generate UI) and you (to
integrate it). Set the project up so that handoff is clean and repeatable:

- Treat `frontend/design-ref/` as the source of truth for visual
  design — colors, type scale, spacing, component look-and-feel. Extract design
  tokens from it into a single shared place (Tailwind config / a tokens file) so
  generated and hand-written components stay consistent.
- Establish and document conventions a Claude-Design-generated component must
  follow to fit our codebase: file/folder layout, naming, how it imports tokens,
  how it receives data (props vs. data-fetching layer), and where API calls live
  (keep them out of presentational components).
- Define the **integration workflow** in writing: when I paste a new component
  from Claude Design, what steps you take to wire it in (place file, swap
  hardcoded values for tokens, connect to the backend, replace the Svelte
  equivalent, verify parity).

## Persist the conventions
Once we agree on the stack and handoff conventions, update `claude.md` (or add a
section) capturing: the target stack, the Svelte→React mapping decisions, the
design-token setup, and the step-by-step Claude Design integration workflow — so
future sessions and handoffs don't have to rediscover this.

## First step
Read everything above and the listed files, then give me your summary and a
proposed order of work. Nothing else yet.